@prefix sh: <http://www.w3.org/ns/shacl#> .
@prefix test: <http://test.example.com/> .
@prefix : <http://test.example.com/shapes/> .

# sh:alternativePath combined with sh:inversePath nesting.
# Per ADR-008 (path-from-string-scope), the new path AST classes
# (Alternative, Inverse) compose by dataclass nesting. This case
# exercises Alternative([Inverse(...), Predicate(...)]) which emits
# `(^<...>|<...>)` SPARQL syntax.
#
# Note on operator choice: we use Alternative + Inverse rather than
# sh:zeroOrMorePath / sh:oneOrMorePath / sh:zeroOrOnePath because
# Virtuoso (the project's HTTP endpoint at localhost:8899) requires
# transitive paths to have a bound starting node. With unbound `?x`,
# Virtuoso raises "transitive start not given". Alternative and
# Inverse do not require anchoring and work on both Virtuoso and
# rdflib in-memory consistently. The Kleene operators (*, +, ?) are
# parser/AST-tested via test_path_ast.py unit tests; full end-to-end
# coverage for them is deferred to environments without the Virtuoso
# anchor constraint (e.g., a future Jena-based endpoint or a fixture
# that explicitly anchors via sh:targetClass + selective queries).
#
# Expected: alternativePath of (^test:toA | test:property0) anchored
# at ClassA targets. ClassA instances having ANY incoming toA (i.e.,
# at least one other ClassA references them) OR having property0:
#   incoming toA: 0 (from 1,3,5,6), 1 (from 0,2,3), 2 (from 3), 3 (from 6),
#                 4 (from 5), 5 (none), 6 (none)
#   property0:    0, 1, 2, 3
#   alternative:  0, 1, 2, 3, 4, 6 → invalid: 5
#
# (Confirmed by inspection of tests/data/test.ttl toA edges.)

:ClassA a sh:NodeShape ;
  sh:targetClass test:ClassA ;
  sh:property [
    sh:path [ sh:alternativePath ( [ sh:inversePath test:toA ] test:property0 ) ] ;
    sh:minCount 1
  ] .
